WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.620
I'm going to let you in on a secret-- not everything you read on the Internet is true.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.589
I know it's shocking, but it's the case.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.819
In fact, it turns out there's a lot of good information and then there's some other that's not.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.370
In trying to figure out which sources are reliable and which ones aren't is actually not always that easy.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.970
But where do we go if we're looking for answers?

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:24.239
Well, it tends to be any more these days, we go to a search engine on the Internet.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:30.420
And Google happens to be one of the best of these, so that's the one that we go to, probably most often.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:31.930
So let's take a look.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:38.420
What would happen if we did a search on Google and we asked it to tell us "Who invented the airplane?"

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:44.439
Okay, you're going to see results that look like this and it's going to get-- you see a number of names there.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.800
And apologies to my friends in Brazil, but since I'm in North Carolina,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.579
I'm going to go with the Wright brothers as the inventors, not Dumont.

00:00:52.579 --> 00:00:56.090
But these are the different sources-- so there's some debate.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.790
And if you look through the list of Google results, you're going to see things that are links that could be reliable.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.776
You're going to see some links that may be fake news, and you're going to see a bunch of ads.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:09.750
And trying to figure out which one is which, again, not always obvious.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:14.970
Also, you're going to see a discussion about this controversy as to who was actually the inventor.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:20.579
So if you're just looking for a quick answer, we really didn't get it there, but we did get a lot of information.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:26.692
Now, it turns out that we have newer technology, AI-based technology-- chatbots

00:01:26.692 --> 00:01:30.720
--that are giving us information in a much different sort of way.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:34.820
One of the new ones that's really taking the Internet by storm these days is called chatGPT.

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:37.379
Let's ask it the same question.

00:01:38.099 --> 00:01:39.900
Okay, "Who invented the airplane?"

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:45.629
And we see a very simple, succinct, authoritative sounding answer.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.160
We don't see a controversy.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.269
We don't see lots of links.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:49.410
We don't see ads.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.300
We don't see fake news.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.239
We don't see--there's nothing to really sort through.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:56.489
It's just the answer that we're looking for.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.260
Well, that's really attractive.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.852
If I can just ask a question and get "the" answer and not have to sort through it? Well, look, who isn't going to go for that?

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:10.289
We're going to, as these chatbots get better and better, rely on them more and more.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.405
Now, this may not be a complete replacement for search engines

00:02:13.405 --> 00:02:16.949
because sometimes I really do want to look at all the different sources and sort through it.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:21.389
But the point is, we're going to start relying-- probably more and more --on these kinds of sources.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.459
But what makes something reliable?

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:30.298
Well, it tends to be that we look for observability, we look for the citation of sources

00:02:30.298 --> 00:02:33.539
and things like that, because some sources we trust more than we do others.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:40.289
But when we're looking at something like a chatbot's answer, we don't get that.

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:42.330
So there's a tradeoff here.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:47.493
Bottom line, though, if you look at these two results, one's really long and I have to sort through

00:02:47.493 --> 00:02:51.930
and pore through all the details and do my own thinking and the other and just tells me is the answer.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.030
What do you think most people are going to go for?

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:55.360
The simple answer.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.110
Now, that sounds great.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.000
What could possibly go wrong?

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:02.580
Let me tell you what could possibly go wrong.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:04.919
Well, so how does this stuff work?

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:14.639
Well, it turns out that we have an AI system and the AI that is behind the chatbot has to get its information from somewhere.

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:22.139
So we use a knowledge base, sometimes many different sources, and this knowledge base, we call a corpus.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:30.717
And that corpus is what we use to train the AI, so that then we can have a user come along here,

00:03:30.717 --> 00:03:34.150
ask a question of the AI, and get an answer back.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:41.080
So this is the model, and that's all fine and good until somebody decides to mess it up.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:45.647
Somebody like this guy comes along and says,

00:03:45.647 --> 00:03:50.325
"Wouldn't it be fun if I corrupted some of the information in this corpus?"

00:03:50.325 --> 00:03:54.850
"What if I introduced a little bit of wrong information and had it mixed in with the good?"

00:03:54.891 --> 00:04:00.580
Because if it came in, which is really, really blatantly wrong information, this guy is going to detect that.

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:04.772
But if we come in with a little bit and just corrupt a little bit of the results,

00:04:04.772 --> 00:04:10.780
then we corrupt a little bit of the answers and this guy ends up with the wrong information.

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:17.499
And as we become more and more relying on these kinds of sources, something like this can be insidious.

00:04:17.649 --> 00:04:23.050
It slips in, and before we know it, we've now made decisions based upon bad information.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:27.550
Now, one of the popular ones of these that I said before is called chatGPT.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:30.039
It's a great resource.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:31.689
And you saw what kind of answer it gave.

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:34.252
I'm not saying that this has ever happened with chatGPT.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:40.450
I'm saying let's take a look at this as an example and see what could happen.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.429
What if this happened in this case?

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:47.410
Well, in the example I gave before, we might get the wrong answer about who invented the airplane.

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:48.939
Not the end of the world.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:51.369
Nobody is harmed by this.

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:58.839
But what could happen if the corpus poisoning case led to something more important, something more insidious?

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:06.557
Let's say I go to chatGPT and say "I want to come up with a household cleaner just using stuff around the house."

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:09.069
Well, we could do that kind of search.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:13.764
And if I do that, I'm going to get back a very simple answer that says,

00:05:13.764 --> 00:05:17.799
you know what, you could use baking soda and vinegar and water and mix them in exactly this way.

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:19.180
It's a nice formula.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:23.380
Again, I didn't have to search a thousand links and figure out which ones to use and which ones don't.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.800
I got a simple authoritative answer right there.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.649
So let's say I mix it up and I clean my house.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:31.070
Great!

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:36.850
However, let's look at the case where a bad guy snuck in a little bit of bad information into the corpus.

00:05:38.288 --> 00:05:45.180
Again, not saying this happened with chatGPT, but I'm saying it's possible with any AI to potentially poison the corpus.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:49.269
And if that were to happen, what if the formula that came out then came out and said,

00:05:49.269 --> 00:05:53.230
instead of using those ingredients, let's mix ammonia and bleach.

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:55.509
Okay, those are two things you have around the house.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:58.209
Well, it turns out that's quite toxic.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:00.459
That's a bad result for you.

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:05.170
And it could result in health problems for the individual that ends up mixing these two things together.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:06.850
So this is just an example.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:12.610
I'm not saying that the world is going to come to an end because somebody mixed up bleach and ammonia.

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:17.386
But imagine that example where this person is making decisions-- important decisions

00:06:17.386 --> 00:06:23.083
--based upon information in their AI, and they become so reliant because this chatbot

00:06:23.083 --> 00:06:26.889
has been so trustworthy for so long that we end up with a problem.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.290
Well, this is not without precedent.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:31.870
In fact, we've had chatbots go rogue before.

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:37.442
There was a case where a chatbot went on the internet, started learning the language of the internet and the way people interacted,

00:06:37.442 --> 00:06:44.260
and within a day it was spouting all kinds of offensive things to people and had to be shut down.

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:52.509
So, again, not everything that we see on the internet is in fact true, and not everything that's true is worth talking about.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:54.579
So there's a filter that has to go on.

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:57.820
So what should we expect of our AI?

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:03.220
Well, we want some sort of observability in order to create this level of trustworthiness.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:04.690
We want to be able to verify.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:11.800
In an ideal world, I'd like for the chatbot to cite its sources so that I can then go to those sources and verify.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:17.320
I'd like to even it, almost in a math way, say "show me your work." Don't just give me the answer.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:21.820
Sometimes we just want the answer, but in some cases we really need it to show its work.

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:24.339
And a lot of times, these systems don't do that.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:26.170
But we're going to need to rely on that.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:29.740
Another example where this could come along is with code samples.

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:35.617
You can go into chatGPT for instance, and it's very good, tell it to write you a particular routine--

00:07:35.617 --> 00:07:40.720
and tell it the language --and it will give you source code. You can copy and paste it into yours.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:42.850
Again, great stuff.

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:50.405
But what if the corpus was poisoned and in fact it inserts malware or a backdoor into your code.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:56.333
If you start relying on that as your source and all you do is say, "write me a code snippet," copy and paste,

00:07:56.333 --> 00:08:02.589
and you don't verify what's happening, you could end up with a program that's a disaster and not even know about it.

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:10.203
So this is what we have to do: It's the old lesson that we've always had when it comes to sources--trust, but verify.

00:08:10.203 --> 00:08:17.439
And don't stop doing it just because it's a computer or just because it's AI, also insist on verification.

00:08:17.439 --> 00:08:21.457
Thanks for watching! Please remember to like this video and

00:08:21.457 --> 00:08:28.086
subscribe to this channel so we can continue to bring you content that matters to you.

